  "cards": [
    {"dot": "cyan", "title": "Three detection contexts", "items": ["Configured capture rules run before filtering; a record omitted from output can still trigger a capture alert (internal/collector/filtering_writer.go:60-81).", "Capture-time mode adds observed scope, ordering and bounded lateness. Default rules retain their separate semantics (docs/RULES_ENGINE.md:45-77).", "Offline packs operate on retained, finalized records using their own strict format, grouped thresholds and coverage report. They cannot recover discarded observations (docs/RULES_ENGINE.md:98-154)."]},
    {"dot": "emerald", "title": "Saved alerts and external delivery are separate", "items": ["deliver-alerts explicitly queues redacted notices; opening the outbox starts no background sender (internal/delivery/outbox.go:52-54; docs/RULES_ENGINE.md:91).", "The SQLite outbox commits notices and retains idempotency receipts. It is pinned to one destination and refuses capacity overflow (outbox.go:77-108,111-159).", "2xx delivers; 408/425/429/5xx and transport errors remain pending; other non-2xx responses are rejected. Reinvoke delivery after the persisted backoff (outbox.go:229-246)."]},
    {"dot": "amber", "title": "What an alert does and does not establish", "items": ["Webhook delivery is at least once: receiver deduplication must use Idempotency-Key (internal/delivery/outbox.go:1-2,224-225).", "An alert is a detection result, not proof of compromise or an inline block. The separate firewall owns enforcement (README.md:45-47,87-89).", "Packet-time network detectors and approved-baseline deviations are other alert sources; the baseline lifecycle has its own diagram (collector.go:896; docs/behavioral-monitoring.md:71-88)."]}
  ]
